# Line endings are LF everywhere, including on Windows.
#
# Without this, git on Windows checks out CRLF by default (core.autocrlf=true).
# That breaks anything comparing a committed file against freshly generated
# bytes: typesafe-gen emits LF, the working copy has CRLF, and the drift test
# reports a file as out of date on Windows and only on Windows. That is exactly
# how this was found — every other platform in the matrix passed.
#
# Go source is LF by convention, cassettes and fixtures are compared byte for
# byte, and a shell script with CRLF does not run. There is no file here that
# wants CRLF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Executable scripts, so the bit survives a checkout.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf

# Generated output. Marking it keeps diffs collapsed by default on GitHub and
# keeps it out of language statistics; it is still reviewed like any file.
*_typesafe.go linguist-generated=true

# Recorded API traffic. Large, mechanical, and not written by hand.
*.jsonl linguist-generated=true
testdata/spec/openapi.json linguist-vendored=true
